\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The two anchors and the interval between them, from the released labels, posterior view. Rostral anchor: the lowest rib-bearing vertebra and the rib that makes it one. Caudal anchor: S1. Both are found from the labels rather than assumed, and panels are coloured by role rather than identifier, since colouring T12 would assert the count the figure derives. Panels (b) and (c) reach the same interval of four from opposite ends and are indistinguishable by it. All panels are at one millimetre scale, aligned at the sacral base.}}{6}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:anchors}{{3}{6}{The two anchors and the interval between them, from the released labels, posterior view. Rostral anchor: the lowest rib-bearing vertebra and the rib that makes it one. Caudal anchor: S1. Both are found from the labels rather than assumed, and panels are coloured by role rather than identifier, since colouring T12 would assert the count the figure derives. Panels (b) and (c) reach the same interval of four from opposite ends and are indistinguishable by it. All panels are at one millimetre scale, aligned at the sacral base}{figure.3}{}}
